;
EXPLORING THE SOFTWARE COLLECTION

A - Exploring the software collection

:A
EXPLORING THE SOFTWARE COLLECTION

The standard P: disk contains the 310 SIG/M and 93 CPMUG volumes. These were
assembled by the CP/M user groups and circulated amongst enthusiasts. They
contain an extraordinary variety of material: programming tools, source code,
games, technical notes, correspondence and programs written for particular
makes of computer.

The individual volumes are supplied as LBR files. An LBR is a library
containing a number of separate files. The individual members may themselves
be compressed.

    DIR SIGM:
    DIR CPMUG:

A directory of numbered libraries doesn't tell you much about their contents.
You can look inside one without downloading it:

    LDIR P0:SIGMV011

LDIR lists the members of the library. To read a text member, supply both the
library name and the member name to LT:

    LT P0:SIGMV011 ABSTRACT.011

Use the actual name shown by LDIR. This is particularly useful when an archive
contains an abstract or instructions, and you're trying to decide whether the
volume is worth downloading.

If you mount a different P: archive, use the DU or named directory belonging
to that disk instead. P0:SIGMV011 is an example for the default archive.

When you find something you want, download the library itself using YSEND and
unpack it on your own machine. There is no general caller facility for
extracting archive members onto the RCP/M disks.

Not everything in a historical archive will run on every CP/M machine. Some
programs are written for a particular screen, disk controller, terminal or
operating-system revision. Read the author's documentation before trying the
program locally. Also bear in mind that the original author may have imposed
conditions on copying or redistribution.
